1. What is Git?
Git: Git is a distributed version control system used to track changes in source code and collaborate with developers.

GitHub: GitHub is a cloud-based platform used to host Git repositories and collaborate on projects.

Difference:
Git	GitHub
Version control tool	Code hosting platform
Works locally	Works online
Tracks code changes	Stores and shares repositories
Open-source	Online platform

///Git Installation & Configuration
# Check Git version
git --version

# Set username
git config --global user.name "Your Name"

# Set email
git config --global user.email "your@email.com"

# Check configuration
git config --list

# Check username
git config user.name

# Check email
git config user.email

# Open Git configuration
git config --global --edit

// Create a Repository
# Initialize Git
git init

# Clone an existing repository
git clone <repository-url>

# Clone into a specific folder
git clone <repository-url> my-project

# Check repository status
git status

// Git Branch Commands
# List branches
git branch

# Create a branch
git branch feature

# Switch branch
git switch feature

# Create and switch branch
git switch -c feature

# Switch to main
git switch main

# Delete a branch
git branch -d feature

# Force delete a branch
git branch -D feature

# Rename current branch
git branch -m new-name

# List remote branches
git branch -r

# List all branches
git branch -a

